{"claims":[
{
"confidence": "medium",
"failing_input": "A cart state created directly by initial world generation with a simulated machine fault input sequence [t=0.00 cargo=150.0,w=1.000] and [t=0.50 clean=1] on a 150 kg load — actually, this is just one experimental data point unless replicated across a pre-specified future sample.",
"file": "src/sim/cart.ts",
"line": 178,
"summary": "One physical data point generated in one particular way cannot establish a general, validated predictive rule for future reloads; a mean is not an experimentally validated safety limit.",
"why": "A single generated outcome provides no empirical distribution, uncertainty, robustness to changed conditions, or evidence that 150 kg is a boundary between safe and unsafe reloads. The numerical result is a hypothesis for replicated testing, not a demonstrated operational criterion."
},
{
"confidence": "high",
"failing_input": "Inputs that satisfy all of this experiment's assumptions but differ in an unmodelled respect (for example future operation at a different ambient temperature) can satisfy the numerical formula while providing no evidence that the future physical outcome is high-quality.",
"file": "src/sim/trebuchet.ts",
"line": 4,
"summary": "An answer dependent on unmeasured conditions cannot be converted into a general real-world predictive claim without stating and validating those conditions.",
"why": "Even a correctly fitted deterministic relationship establishes only a conditional model result. Extrapolating it beyond its tested domain requires additional evidence about the omitted variables and domain."
}
]}
